{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain`, `NodeContents`, and `SourceNodes` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, and evidence; `graph::validator`\n  runs criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.public_api`. Each obligation maps to an asserting test, as follows.\n\n**One defining module path, no public re-exports.**\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter. It fails on any `use_declaration` with a bare `pub` visibility modifier. It also asserts that each file parses without errors.\n- `fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` runs `cargo doc` fresh and walks the rustdoc export tables. Its helper `documented_exports` asserts that no path appears twice (`Duplicate rustdoc export`).\n\n**The inventory links each item to its owning requirement, and the module list is consistent.**\n- `public_exports_match_the_appendix` computes the set difference between the specified exports (paths plus item kinds, from the inventory) and the documented rustdoc exports. It fails on any missing or unexpected item. This covers the `domain`, `graph`, `request`, `agent`, `sources`, `outline` and `storage` modules, along with their submodules.\n- The helper `documented_exports` panics when an exported path has no inventory declaration.\n- Doc-hidden internals are not listed in rustdoc, so they are not mistaken for SDK exports.\n\n**The owning requirement describes the item's purpose and declares its public Rust interface; a change to the interface requires a change to the requirement.**\n- `check_declaration` asserts that each owning requirement has a non-empty description and a Rust listing.\n- It compares the trait impls, the indexed member names, and the declaration. The declaration comparison covers fields, variants, and inherent methods, using `signature_parts` on both the requirement and the rustdoc output. The comparison fails in both directions (\"Only in requirements\" and \"Only in Rustdoc\").\n- `fn[member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed]` validates the comparison helper itself. It shows that reordering methods is accepted. It also shows that a changed field type, return type, or receiver mutability is rejected, as is `(T,)` versus `(T)`.\n\n**Every supported struct with public fields is `#[non_exhaustive]`.** `check_declaration` asserts that any struct page with `structfield` anchors has `#[non_exhaustive]` in its published declaration. It runs for every exported item, so the check is not limited to a sample.\n\n**Constructors take the needed values, builders are consuming and take concrete values.** The signature comparison above enforces these shapes for every item. Behavior is exercised by:\n- `agent/config.rs` `fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]`, for the `Bedrock`, `OpenAi`, `Ollama` and credentials builders.\n- `outline/selector.rs` `fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]`, for `Segment::new` and `ordinal`.\n- `tests/outline_content.rs` `fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]`, for `OutlineItem::new` and `Outline::new`.\n- The two `tests/source_defaults.rs` tests, `fn[scoped_text_results_keep_line_numbers_context_and_completeness]` and `fn[source_bytes_error_kinds_and_language_names_round_trip]`, for `TextQuery`, `StructuralQuery` and `SourceFile`.\n\n**Optional fields start as `None`.**\n- `agent/config.rs` `fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` serializes freshly constructed configs and sees no optional keys.\n- `tests/outline_content.rs` `fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` asserts `item.name_span == None` and `outline.diagnostic.is_none()`.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs` `fn[scoped_text_results_keep_line_numbers_context_and_completeness]` asserts `query.limit == None`.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs` `fn[source_bytes_error_kinds_and_language_names_round_trip]` asserts `file.language == None`.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs` `fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` asserts `StructuralQuery.limit == None`.\n- `outline/selector.rs` `fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` asserts `Segment.ordinal == None`.\n- `Record::new` takes its version as an explicit argument, so it has no default to test.\n\n**Backend inference options start empty and are added one key at a time.** `fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` shows no `options` key on a fresh config. It then adds options one key at a time with `.option(...)` and asserts the merged `{\"a\":2,\"b\":true}`, which includes replacement of a repeated key. It repeats the single-key additions for OpenAI and Ollama.\n\n**Implementation details are not part of the public API.** The no-extra-exports half of the set-difference check covers this. The tests flag any undocumented export that appears in the rustdoc output.\n\nI found no behavior that violates the requirement and that these tests would accept. The default-`None` and constructor behavior checks are samples across the structs rather than exhaustive. The shape of every struct, constructor and builder is still checked against the owning requirement by the rustdoc comparison. A struct with a wrong default would fall to its own chapter's requirement tests.",
  "references": "83e5a024def69d2c249a2488b0d6e064785b414d91c24616a0f007d173d67065",
